<p class="text-lg text-silver-400 leading-relaxed mb-6">
  A lot of EV shopping advice is still written as if a federal tax credit waits at the end of the deal. For a vehicle acquired after September 30, 2025, it does not. The new clean vehicle credit, the used clean vehicle credit, and the commercial clean vehicle credit stopped for vehicles acquired after that date. The IRS states the cutoff at <a href="https://www.irs.gov/clean-vehicle-tax-credits" rel="noopener">irs.gov/clean-vehicle-tax-credits</a>. What remains, and it is not nothing, is a patchwork of state rebates, utility rates, and the ordinary question of whether this car’s range and charging fit the driving you do. This page is that patchwork, with the credit guide linked for the details that change by state.
</p>

<h2>The federal credit, stated carefully</h2>
<p>
  Through September 30, 2025, a qualifying new clean vehicle could be worth a credit of up to $7,500 and a qualifying used clean vehicle a credit of up to $4,000, subject to income, price, assembly, and battery-content rules that already kept many cars out. “Acquired” meant a written binding contract and a payment. A car you buy now is outside that window. A dealer who still prints “includes the $7,500 tax credit” on a car acquired after the cutoff is describing history, or describing a discount the dealer is funding and calling by the old name. Ask which one it is, and ask to see it as a line on the buyer’s order rather than as a tax concept.
</p>
<p>
  The home-charger credit had its own end date. Do not assume a wall-connector installation is subsidized. Confirm the current IRS position before you put the charger in the construction budget. Our running account of what replaced the federal credit, verified against state program sites, is <a href="/guides/ev/ev-tax-credits/">EV tax credits and rebates in 2026</a>. A state-by-state look at the same programs is <a href="/blog/state-ev-incentives-2026/">state EV incentives</a>. Programs run out of money and pause. The official page, not a blog summary, is the one to check the week you buy.
</p>

<h2>What it costs to drive one</h2>
<p>
  Electricity is priced in cents per kilowatt-hour, and the number that matters is the one on your utility bill at the hour you will charge, not the national average in an ad. Time-of-use rates often make overnight charging the cheap window and late-afternoon charging the expensive one. A public DC fast charger is a different product with a different price, sometimes by the minute rather than by the kilowatt-hour, and it is the wrong basis for judging a car you will charge at home. The U.S. Department of Energy’s fuel-economy site compares gasoline and electricity costs for specific vehicles at <a href="https://www.fueleconomy.gov/" rel="noopener">fueleconomy.gov</a>. Use it with your own utility rate typed in.
</p>
<p>
  Range on the window sticker is a laboratory figure. Cold weather, highway speed, a roof box, and a low state of charge at which you are unwilling to arrive all shrink it. The honest test is the route you drive in a week, not the route in the commercial. If you cannot charge at home or at work, an EV is a different and usually worse ownership experience, because you are buying the public-charging price every week. That is a reason to pause, not a moral failing.
</p>
<p>
  Maintenance is lighter on brakes and on engine oil, and it is not free. Tires wear, cabin filters still exist, and a battery that falls below the warranty threshold is a warranty question with a specific capacity percentage and a specific term in the booklet. Read that term before you rely on “the battery is warranted for eight years.” Some of those warranties pay for capacity loss. Some pay only for failure. They are written differently by manufacturer.
</p>

<h2>Insurance, repair, and the VIN</h2>
<p>
  Insurers price an EV from the same inputs as any other car, plus repair cost and, in some cases, battery replacement cost after a crash. A higher sticker and a more expensive body repair can raise the premium even when the driver record is unchanged. Compare quotes on the VIN rather than on the slogan that EVs are cheaper or more expensive to insure. The <a href="/guides/insurance/">insurance hub</a> is the general method. There is no EV exception to shopping more than one carrier.
</p>
<p>
  Collision repair often requires a shop the manufacturer recognizes, because the battery has to be made safe before ordinary body work. Ask your insurer whether the shop you want is authorized for that make. A car that cannot be repaired locally is a car that spends longer in a rental, and rental coverage is a line on the policy you can choose before the crash.
</p>
<p>
  The VIN still tells you what you bought. Battery capacity, motor configuration, and charging hardware are equipment, and sellers mix them up. Decode the VIN, then match the charge-port type and the onboard-charger rating to the plug you intend to use. NHTSA’s product catalog is what our <a href="/vin-decoder/">VIN decoder</a> queries. Recalls apply to EVs on the same terms as any other vehicle. A high-voltage or software campaign is still a free dealer repair. Check it at the <a href="/recall-checker/">recall checker</a>.
</p>

<h2>Buying one used</h2>
<p>
  A used EV adds a battery-health question the odometer does not answer. Ask for a state-of-health reading from the manufacturer’s tool or from a shop that can pull it, and ask what the warranty has left in both time and miles. DC fast charging as the only charging history is something to ask about. It is not, by itself, proof the battery is ruined. It is a reason to want the health reading rather than a reassurance.
</p>
<p>
  The federal used clean vehicle credit is subject to the same acquisition cutoff. Do not let a seller price a 2026 purchase as if the credit still attaches. State rebates sometimes include used vehicles and sometimes do not. The <a href="/guides/ev/ev-tax-credits/">incentives guide</a> separates those. Title brands matter at least as much as on a gasoline car, because a flood-damaged high-voltage battery is a dangerous repair. Read the <a href="/guides/title/salvage-title-guide/">salvage-title guide</a> if the history report is anything other than clean.
</p>
<p>
  Home charging is an electrical job. A dedicated circuit, a correctly rated connector, and a permit where your city requires one are part of the purchase even though they are not on the buyer’s order. A dryer outlet and a long extension cord is how people have fires. If you rent, get the landlord’s answer in writing before you rely on overnight charging to make the range work.
</p>

<h2>A reasonable order</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Confirm the federal credit does not apply to a vehicle acquired after September 30, 2025, unless you are in the narrow case the IRS still describes.</li>
  <li>Check the state and utility program the week you buy, on the official site.</li>
  <li>Price electricity at your own rate and at the hours you will charge.</li>
  <li>Decode the VIN, check recalls, and get a battery-health reading on a used car.</li>
  <li>Quote insurance on that VIN before you treat the payment as the cost of owning it.</li>
</ol>

